    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Reserves vs. reality
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s happening?</strong> The International Energy Agency (IEA) and its 32 member countries have agreed to <a href="https://www.euronews.com/business/2026/03/11/germany-and-japan-to-tap-oil-reserves-as-iea-and-g7-weigh-record-release" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">release 400Mn barrels of oil</a> from strategic reserves. The release, more than double the size of the emergency release following the Russian invasion of Ukraine, is intended to offset shortages, calm market fears, and reduce surging fuel values. Implementation timelines will vary by country depending on their national reserve policies.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>United G7:</strong> The G7 nations presented a united political front in response to the oil market disruption caused by the Iran war. During emergency discussions, leaders emphasized the need to stabilize global energy markets by increasing production and avoiding export restrictions. This political backing helped pave the way for the IEA’s decision to release the oil reserve.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Meanwhile:</strong> Brent crude rose <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c1w5141vx53o" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">more than 9% to exceed $100 per barrel</a>, reflecting persistent fears of supply disruption. The spike comes as tensions escalated in the Strait of Hormuz, where Iran has intensified attacks on shipping. Despite the historic reserve release, traders remain concerned that prolonged conflict and restricted shipping through the key oil corridor could keep global energy values elevated.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
