<mj-section background-color="#eff1f4" padding="1px 0.5px 1px 1px" border-radius="5px">
  <mj-column background-color="#fff" border-radius="5px" padding="0px">
    
    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    Orbán war gamble
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Summary: Hungary-Ukraine tensions escalate as Orbán frames election around war, energy, and security.</strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What’s happening?</strong> Tensions have escalated between Hungary and Ukraine ahead of Hungary’s parliamentary election. The government of Viktor Orbán accuses Kyiv of <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/crm8rxwyyz8o" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">attempting to undermine his re-election</a> through threats, sabotage, and disruptions to oil delivery via the Druzhba pipeline. The dispute intensified after Russian drone strikes damaged infrastructure near the pipeline, halting oil deliveries to Hungary.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Ukraine’s stance:</strong> Ukraine has strongly rejected accusations from Viktor Orbán's government. Officials say the disruption to oil delivery through the Druzhba pipeline was caused by damage from Russian drone strikes near the Brody oil hub. Ukrainian leaders have also accused Budapest of fueling anti-Ukraine rhetoric to influence domestic politics ahead of the election.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>On the ground:</strong> Polls show that Viktor Orbán’s Fidesz Party is trailing the opposition Tisza Party. Across Hungary, government messaging frames the election as a choice between “peace” under Fidesz and the risk of war if opposition leader Péter Magyar wins. Billboards and campaign materials link Magyar with Volodymyr Zelenskyy and accuse the opposition of supporting pro-war policies in the EU.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
<mj-spacer height="10px" />
    </mj-section>
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
