        >
          <h2
            style="
              padding-bottom: 8px;
              color: #06266d;
              text-align: left;
              border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d;
              font-size: 26px;
              line-height: 1.2;
              font-weight: 300;
            "
          >
            Analysis
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          padding="10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="#000000"
        >
          <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  Drone warfare era
</h2>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Summary: Iran’s drone-heavy retaliation signals shift toward mass, low-cost drone warfare globally.     </strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What’s happening?</strong> Iran launched a large-scale retaliatory air campaign after the US-Israeli strikes under Operation Epic Fury killed Supreme Leader Ayatollah Ali Khamenei. The strikes primarily targeted Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) states and relied heavily on mass waves of one-way attack drones, particularly Shahed-series systems, supported by ballistic and cruise missiles. Data shows that <a href="https://www.csis.org/analysis/unpacking-irans-drone-campaign-gulf-early-lessons-future-drone-warfare" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">drones made up roughly 71% of all recorded strikes</a>.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Implications:</strong> Ukraine’s experience demonstrates that defending against mass drone attacks requires a scalable, layered approach. The US must prioritize low-cost interceptor drones to counter mass drone attacks while preserving high-end missiles for advanced threats. Effective defense also requires integration into doctrine, specialized counter-UAS units, strong sensor-fusion systems, resilient communications, onboard AI, and rapid iteration of tactics and technology.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Big picture:</strong> Mass, low-cost drones are becoming central tools for sustained military pressure. This model favors actors with strong production capacity and adaptable operational doctrine. For militaries like the US, the challenge is adapting defenses to this reality.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
