          >
            Foundations
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
    What is the domino theory?
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border="1px solid #00000013"
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>

<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What is it?</strong> The domino theory is a concept from the Cold War that said if one nation fell to communism, nearby countries would too, similar to dominoes. Former US President Dwight D. Eisenhower first cited the idea as the “<a href="https://link.geopoliticalsummary.com/gs-21-oct-25-foundations-h1-14-falling-domino-principle?source=website" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">falling domino principle</a>” in 1954. There was the concern that if Vietnam could turn communist, then other Asian countries would quickly follow suit.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Impact:</strong> This theory was used to justify military measures, alliances, and aid programs that were geared towards stopping the spread of communism. The theory’s credibility was later challenged as critics argued that it oversimplified local dynamics and exaggerated the cohesion among communist movements.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;">For example, after the Vietnam War, a couple of communist movements grew stronger in Asia, such as Cambodia and Laos, but many others did not fall to communism.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Big picture:</strong> The domino theory illustrates how fear of different ideas influenced international relations. The theory led to expensive and controversial measures to keep the global balance, which weren't always justified. The outcome of the Vietnam War showed the weakness of the theory.</p>
</mj-text>
